# power-covered-by: TestASnapshotReadKeepsItsLogEntry -- a DIRECTED test that drives BOTH sides of D-A7-5's boundary through onClient: a plain read must take the read-index path, and a read naming a remembered timestamp must not. The plain half is the contrast that keeps the assertion from passing over a path nothing took, which is this register's commonest entry
# power-refutation: this class named TestRaftExitCriteria, a 10,000-seed sweep costing 19 hours per invocation at the exit run's measured 6.9 s/seed. Seven mutants named it, so make mutants -- which is in the ci target -- asked for six days for that one covering test, and its baseline timed out and reported INVALID. A covering test built on a sweep stops covering when the workload moves and does it silently; this one is directed and cannot
# mutates: D-A7-5's BOUNDARY, planted as a defect. A read naming a timestamp is served by read index instead of by a log entry, so it stages no read mark. Ansh, ruling 11: 'the mutant is the design decision itself planted as a defect -- a snapshot read served by read index. That makes the boundary between the two read paths something the suite kills rather than something the code remembers.' The failure is BUG-022 returning as a design consequence rather than as a patch: PrewriteInto's third first-committer-wins guard consults a record that does not exist, reads it as 'nobody has looked', and a commit lands below an answer the database already gave. Silent -- no error, no divergence, no structural invariant violated: a well-formed database with money missing from it, on a schedule with no faults in it. It is killed by the bank's CONSERVATION check rather than by any structural oracle, which is what BUG-022 actually looked like
